::STAT_U01_L06_GP_Q01::
Question ID: STAT_U01_L06_GP_Q01
MLA Standard: MLA.STAT.DVD.3
Question:
A relative frequency table shows Group A: yes 40%, no 60%. How long should the yes segment be in a 100% segmented bar?
{
~A. 60% of the bar#60% represents the no category.
~B. 100% of the bar#The whole bar represents all responses, not one category.
~C. 20% of the bar#20% is not listed in the table.
=D. 40% of the bar#Segment length matches the relative frequency for that category.
}

::STAT_U01_L06_GP_Q02::
Question ID: STAT_U01_L06_GP_Q02
MLA Standard: MLA.STAT.DVD.3
Question:
Why are relative frequencies useful when comparing two groups of different sizes?
{
=A. They compare proportions instead of raw counts#Proportions make comparisons fair when totals differ.
~B. They remove the need for categories#Categories are still compared.
~C. They make every group have the same raw count#Relative frequency changes the form of comparison, not the actual counts.
~D. They prove one category caused another#Relative frequencies do not establish causation.
}

::STAT_U01_L06_GP_Q03::
Question ID: STAT_U01_L06_GP_Q03
MLA Standard: MLA.STAT.DVD.3
Question:
In a segmented bar graph, what should the total length of each bar represent?
{
=A. 100% of that group#Each segmented bar represents the whole group split into category parts.
~B. The largest category only#The largest category is one segment, not the whole bar.
~C. The raw count in the smallest group#The bar is scaled to the group total, usually 100%.
~D. The number of categories squared#The number of categories does not determine total bar length.
}

::STAT_U01_L06_GP_Q04::
Question ID: STAT_U01_L06_GP_Q04
MLA Standard: MLA.STAT.DVD.3
Question:
A table shows Grade 9: walk 30%, bus 50%, car 20%. Which segment should be longest?
{
~A. Walk#Walk is 30%, smaller than bus.
=B. Bus#Bus has the largest relative frequency at 50%.
~C. Car#Car is 20%, the smallest of the three.
~D. All segments are equal#The percentages are not equal.
}

::STAT_U01_L06_GP_Q05::
Question ID: STAT_U01_L06_GP_Q05
MLA Standard: MLA.STAT.DVD.3
Question:
A segmented bar graph uses the same category order in every bar. Why is this important?
{
~A. It changes the relative frequencies#Order does not change the data values.
~B. It makes every segment equal#Segment size still depends on percentage.
=C. It makes comparisons across groups clearer#Consistent category order prevents misreading segment positions.
~D. It removes the need for labels#Labels are still needed to identify categories.
}
